>> B2: Describe how file descriptors are associated with open files.
>> Are file descriptors unique within the entire OS or just within a
>> single process?

Each process maintains its own list of file descriptors (stored in thread_current()->open_fdriptors).
When a file is opened using syscall_open(), a new open_fd structure is allocated, containing 
 a unique fd, a pointer to open file and a list elem to maintain a internal lists of fd. and the 
 open_fd structure is added to the current process’s file descriptor list. when a file is accessed, the
 fd is open_fd structure is added to the current process’s file descriptor list.

File descriptors are unique within a single process but not globally unique across the OS. each process
has its own independent set of fds. two different process can open same file and receive different fds.
if a process forks or creates a child process, the child does not inherit its parent’s file descriptors.


