---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B7: The "exec" system call returns -1 if loading the new executable
>> fails, so it cannot return before the new executable has completed
>> loading.  How does your code ensure this?  How is the load
>> success/failure status passed back to the thread that calls "exec"?

In process_execute(), the parent process waits using sema_down(&pcb->sema_initial), 
blocking until the child process signals completion.

In start_process(), the child attempts to load the executable. If successful, it 
assigns its pid and calls sema_up(&pcb->sema_initial). If loading fails, it sets 
pcb->pid = PID_ERROR before signaling.

>> B8: Consider parent process P with child process C.  How do you
>> ensure proper synchronization and avoid race conditions when P
>> calls wait(C) before C exits?  After C exits?  How do you ensure
>> that all resources are freed in each case?  How about when P
>> terminates without waiting, before C exits?  After C exits?  Are
>> there any special cases?

1. P blocks on sema_wait in process_wait(), ensuring it waits until C terminates.
When C calls process_exit(), it signals sema_up, allowing P to resume execution and collect the exit status.

2. Since C has already exited, P finds C marked exited == true and retrieves the exit code immediately.
The child’s Process Control Block (PCB) is then freed.

3. C becomes orphaned, but its PCB remains allocated so C can exit cleanly.
In process_exit(), P iterates over its child list, marking them as orphans.

4. The exit code was already collected by P.
P frees all remaining child PCBs in process_exit().

special: Double wait: If P calls wait(C) twice, the second call fails.


